* SKY130 two-stage Miller-compensated operational amplifier.
*
* Circuit B of the case study. Topology is the textbook two-stage Miller amplifier as posed by
* Hershenson, Boyd and Lee (IEEE TCAD 20(1):1-21, 2001), which is the canonical sizing benchmark in
* this literature; this deck, its parameterisation and its testbenches are our own.
*
* WHY THIS CIRCUIT IS A FAIR SECOND TEST. It is structurally independent of the bandgap of Circuit A:
* the objective is open-loop gain rather than supply rejection, there is no PTAT core, no start-up
* branch and no temperature-coefficient specification, and the amplifier runs OPEN loop, so no
* Tian probe and no loop-cut is needed. What it shares with Circuit A is only the process, the
* evaluator and the protocol -- which is the point.
*
* SEVEN design variables, matching the dimension of Circuit A's base case so the two rankings are
* compared at equal D. The bias current is a fixed ideal source at 10 uA: that is the same modelling
* choice declared for Circuit A -- it isolates amplifier sizing from current-reference design -- and
* it is disclosed, not hidden.
*
* WHY AN NMOS INPUT PAIR, AND NOT THE PMOS PAIR OF THE FIRST DRAFT. Measured on this PDK at L = 1 um,
* tt, 27 C: |Vth| = 1.033 V for pfet_01v8 against 0.615 V for nfet_01v8. A PMOS pair therefore needs
* an input common mode below Vdd - |Vth| - Vdsat = 1.8 - 1.03 - 0.2 = 0.57 V, so at the mid-rail
* common mode of 0.9 V the pair sits below threshold, the tail device drops into its linear region and
* the whole amplifier collapses -- which is exactly what the first version of this deck did
* (Id = 0.157 uA in the input pair, Vds = 2.2 mV across the tail). An NMOS pair needs only
* Vcm > Vth + Vdsat = 0.82 V, so mid-rail works with margin. Recorded here because it is a property
* of this process, not a preference.
*
* MEASUREMENT NOTES, inherited from the bandgap deck and re-verified here:
*   - `meas ac ... FIND v(x)` returns the REAL PART of a complex vector. Build a real magnitude
*     first: `let vo = mag(v(out))`.
*   - `vdb()` inside `meas` aborts the whole AC analysis in ngspice-46. Do not use it.
*   - `.meas op` is rejected; operating-point values come from `op` + `print`, one probe per line so
*     that a single unsupported probe cannot abort the rest.
*   - `let` vectors are plot-scoped: keep arithmetic inside the plot that produced its inputs.
*   - run `ac` BEFORE any `dc` sweep, which leaves the simulator at the sweep's last point.
*
* The differential drive is set up so that |v(out)| IS the open-loop differential gain: Vip and Vin
* apply +0.5 and -0.5 V of AC about the common-mode point, so the differential input is exactly
* 1 V and no division is needed. An NMOS pair with a PMOS mirror load inverts, so phase margin is
* taken as 180 deg minus the phase ACCUMULATED between 100 Hz and the crossing, which is independent
* of that polarity. Vdd carries AC 1 as well, but the two stimuli are never armed at the same time --
* the evaluator zeroes one before sweeping the other, exactly as it does for the bandgap's PSRR and
* loop-gain phases.

.title SKY130 two-stage Miller operational amplifier
* ${CORNER} is 'tt' for every OPTIMIZATION run; the placeholder exists so the process-and-supply
* pass can re-simulate the SAME deck at ss/ff/sf/fs without a second copy of the netlist.
* ${VDD_DC}: 1.8 V nominal, 1.62/1.98 for the +/-10 % sweep.
.lib ${SKY130_LIB} ${CORNER}

* ===== the seven design variables =====
.param w1=${W1}        $ input pair M1/M2 width, um
.param w3=${W3}        $ PMOS mirror load M3/M4 width, um
.param w5=${W5}        $ NMOS tail sink and bias mirror width, um
.param w6=${W6}        $ second-stage PMOS driver width, um
.param w7=${W7}        $ second-stage NMOS sink load width, um
.param l_all=${L_ALL}  $ common channel length, um
.param cc=${CC}        $ Miller compensation capacitance, pF

* ===== fixed bias reference: an ideal 10 uA source, mirrored by w5 =====
* Same declared choice as Circuit A: the bias current is ideal so that what is being optimized is
* amplifier sizing and not current-reference design.
.param ibias=10

Vdd vdd 0 DC ${VDD_DC} AC 1

* common mode at mid-rail; +/-0.5 V AC gives exactly 1 V differential
Vcm cm  0  DC '0.5*${VDD_DC}'
Vip inp cm DC 0 AC  0.5
Vin inn cm DC 0 AC -0.5

* ---- bias mirror: 10 uA into an NMOS diode, mirrored to the tail and to the stage-2 sink ----
Ib   vdd nb  dc 'ibias*1u'
XMb  nb  nb  0   0   sky130_fd_pr__nfet_01v8 W={w5} L={l_all}

* ---- stage 1: NMOS input pair, PMOS mirror load ----
XM5  tail nb  0   0   sky130_fd_pr__nfet_01v8 W={w5} L={l_all}
XM1  n1   inp tail 0  sky130_fd_pr__nfet_01v8 W={w1} L={l_all}
XM2  no1  inn tail 0  sky130_fd_pr__nfet_01v8 W={w1} L={l_all}
XM3  n1   n1  vdd vdd sky130_fd_pr__pfet_01v8 W={w3} L={l_all}
XM4  no1  n1  vdd vdd sky130_fd_pr__pfet_01v8 W={w3} L={l_all}

* ---- stage 2: PMOS driver with an NMOS current-sink load ----
* Zero-systematic-offset condition, from M6's gate sitting at the M3/M4 mirror potential and M7
* mirroring Mb:  W6/W3 = 2 * W7/W5.
XM6  out  no1 vdd vdd sky130_fd_pr__pfet_01v8 W={w6} L={l_all}
XM7  out  nb  0   0   sky130_fd_pr__nfet_01v8 W={w7} L={l_all}

* ---- Miller compensation, no nulling resistor: the right-half-plane zero it leaves behind is
* ---- exactly what the phase-margin specification has to pay for, which is the trade-off the
* ---- optimizer is being asked to make.
Cc no1 out 'cc*1p'

* ---- a light load, so the measured bandwidth is a property of the amplifier and not of an
* ---- arbitrarily large capacitor. Fixed, not optimized.
Cl out 0 1p

.end
